## Failure paths vs the fixed revision c89e93fe

Common baseline: count=5, sha256=1c2acf93... before each run. The test used
is "two generations of the same inputs produce byte-identical artifacts"
(filtered with `-t "byte-identical"`), which enters runHaxe five times; the
stub window is the duration of each haxe generation.

(a) Interrupt mid-run — SIGINT to the bun test PID while the stub was live
    (poller confirmed count=0 before signalling):
    rc=1 (test failed), after: count=5, hash 1c2acf93... — INTACT.
    evidence/03-fixed-sigint.log

(b) Timeout — `timeout -s TERM 25 bun test ...` (CI-runner-style kill at a
    fixed deadline; the full test takes ~165-400 s so the stub was long
    live): rc=124, after: count=5, hash 1c2acf93... — INTACT.
    evidence/04-fixed-timeout-sigterm.log

(c) Throw from the probe path — `--preload` replaced `Bun.spawn` with a
    function that throws (runHaxe calls it inside the try, after the stub
    write). Stack trace shows the throw propagating out of runHaxe
    (package-artifacts.test.ts:75). No test file was edited. rc=1,
    after: count=5, hash 1c2acf93... — INTACT.
    evidence/05-fixed-forced-throw.log (preload: /tmp/tfp-force-throw.ts)

(d) Residual limit — SIGKILL. At c89e93fe, `--preload` made `Bun.spawn`
    return a never-resolving `exited` (equivalent to haxe hanging), so the
    stub was provably live (in-process trace: equals_count=0 at the spawn);
    SIGKILL to the bun PID: rc=137, after: count=**0**, hash
    **4f910e40...** — CORRUPTED, as predicted: no handler runs on SIGKILL.
    Restored afterwards with `git checkout HEAD --` (cleanup only, not part
    of the fix, and explicitly not counted as proof).
    evidence/07-c89e93fe-sigkill.log

## Honest limits and what was not verified

- The SIGKILL demonstration at c89e93fe was performed in a dedicated
  worktree (`git worktree` at c89e93fe), because the main tree's HEAD moved
  to 8613334d (2aadcb69) during the investigation. Same file content, same
  code revision — but not the main checkout.
- Machine reset / power loss was not exercised (same class as SIGKILL: no
  handler runs); not separately demonstrated.
- Four earlier SIGKILL attempts in the main tree failed to catch the stub
  window (three because 2aadcb69 had already removed the stubbing and no
  window existed any more; one raced past it). Their logs are retained
  (evidence/06-...) for honesty; the conclusive SIGKILL run is 07.
- bun's signal behaviour (finally running on SIGINT/SIGTERM) is empirical
  for bun 1.3.13; other runners may not run handlers on TERM. The
  `finally` guarantee is a language guarantee only for throw/return; for
  signals it depends on the runtime delivering them as errors/exits that
  unwind JS frames. That dependency is exactly why (d) matters.
- Not verified: the other callers/stub sites elsewhere in the suite at
  revisions before 2aadcb69; scope here is package-artifacts.test.ts only.

## Verdict

The proof owed by the ruling is delivered for the revision it governs
(c89e93fe / 4cf3165d): on every failure path a handler can run — throw,
interrupt, timeout — the tracked file's contents after the failure are
identical (same sha256, same count) to before entering the test. The fix
does NOT cover SIGKILL/machine reset, demonstrated concretely (file left
corrupted). As of 2aadcb69 the mutation no longer exists at all, so the
hazard is eliminated rather than merely guarded.
